// Bounded quantification, enforced. SPEC has always said a bound is what lets a body call a contract's
// methods on a type-parameter value; until opaque type parameters existed there was nothing to check it
// against, so a generic could call whatever it liked and the error surfaced at each CONSUMER's use site
// instead of at the declaration that was wrong.
//
// The message has to name the fix, and the fix is not the one "no such method" would suggest: nothing is
// missing from `T` — the promise is.
fn void neverCalled<T>(T x) {
    x.compareTo(other: x);     // ERROR: `T` is unbounded, so it promises nothing
}
fn int32 main() { return 0; }
